  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 319 AM EST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nToday through Tuesday...\n\nEarly morning observations reveal cloudy skies with temperatures in \nthe low-mid 50s.  Areas of drizzle and light rain continue across \nthe region as a mid-level wave slowly works northeast across the \nregion.  Through sunrise, expect low clouds and drizzle/light rain \ncontinuing.  Lows for the overnight period will likely be in the \nlower 50s.\n\nFor today, aforementioned mid-level wave will continue to move off \nto the northeast.  Light rain and drizzle will likely linger into \nthe mid-morning hours before diminishing around lunchtime.  Cloud \ncover will limit temps north of the I-64 corridor with readings in \nthe low-mid 60s.  Some partial clearing will work into southern KY \nwhere temps along/south of the WK/BG Parkways will likely warm into \nthe upper 60s/lower 70s.  Dry conditions are expected overnight with \nlows around 50 in the I-75 corridor.  Elsewhere, mid-upper 50s are \nexpected.\n\nMoving into Tuesday, dry weather is expected to continue across the \nregion.  Mid-level heights will build across the region in advance \nof a larger upper level trough that will approach the area from the \nwest.  A tightening surface pressure gradient will be noted and \nbreezy southwest winds are expected to push temperatures into the \nmid-upper 70s.  Given the strong warm advection scheme, it is \npossible that the models may underdo temps a bit, so a few spots \ncould touch 80 and Lexington continues to have a chance to break a \nrecord high (77F).  Winds may gust to 25-30 mph at times Tuesday \nafternoon.\n\nTuesday night through Sunday...\n\nHeading into Tuesday night and Wednesday, a large upper level low \nand associated trough axis will roll eastward across the Plains and \ninto the Midwest/Ohio Valley.  Associated with this trough, a nearly \nnorth-south oriented surface cold front will plow eastward across \nKentucky Wednesday morning.  Ahead of the front, a fairly strong low-\nlevel jet axis will strengthen to around 55-60kts and bring a fair \namount of moisture into the region.  Model soundings show PWATs \ngreater than 1.75 inches which is well above climatological norms \nfor early November.  A band of moderate to heavy rain showers will \naccompany the front as it slides through the region.  While ample \nshear will be in place across the region, the early arrival of the \nfront in the daytime, combined with plentiful cloud cover will \nresult in very limited instability.  In terms of sensible weather, \nWednesday will be a dreary, breezy, and rainy day with precipitation \nending by Wednesday night.  Highs on the day will be in the upper \n60s to around 70.  Rainfall amounts of 1-2 inches will be possible \nas this weather feature moves through. Cooler air will filter in \nWednesday night with lows falling into the upper 40s west of I-65, \nand lower 50s elsewhere.\n\nFor Thursday, the upper low will move from the Midwest into the \nGreat Lakes and much of the Ohio and Tennessee Valley will be in the \nmid-level dry slot that should keep us precipitation free.  Highs \nwill be a little cooler with readings in the upper 60s and lows in \nthe low-mid 40s.  Another upper wave will pivot through the region \non Friday, though moisture may be rather scant here and the wave may \njust bring more cloud cover than anything else.  We'll be in between \nweather systems on Saturday with dry conditions and highs in the \nlower 60s.  Another compact upper low will dive southeast out of the \nMidwest on Sunday and may bring some rain showers to the area toward \nthe end of the forecast period.",
